            "scope": "Historical comparative background, not a chronology of Chinese legislation. Claims that business ethics in China had yet to be cultivated, judgment enforcement was immature, and many disputes used non-legal means are the 2009 textbook's characterizations, not current findings.",
            "summary": "Explains how merchant tribunals and lex mercatoria entered English and U.S. common law, then contrasts reputation-based enforcement in Western jurisdictions with the source's assessment of commercial dispute resolution in China.",
            "context_excerpt_ids": []
          },
          "hit_chunk_id": "chk_3af200c915ae90_19_v2",
          "hit_text": "In the US, merchants had tried to avoid the time-consuming court procedures for resolving commercial disputes until the end of the 18th century. Similar to the situation in England, the outcome of the competition between courts of law and merchants’ tribunals was that the customs of merchants were absorbed by judges and integrated into the common law.\n\nThe experience in Western jurisdictions shows that merchants’ concern about their goodwill and long-term reputation, rather than the effectiveness of the court system, is probably the biggest driving force behind the enforceability of contracts. In China, however, business ethics have yet to be cultivated. The deterrent force of peer pressure on contract breakers remains to be seen. Coupled with the immature system of the enforcement of court judgments, it is hardly surprising that many commercial disputes in China are resolved by non-legal means.\n\nIn the next section of the unit, you will find out more about the key milestones in the historical development of Chinese contract law.",
